The purchase callback must update transaction state only. Premium should unlock after the purchase is verified and the entitlement is recorded idempotently.

Assuming the existing package is Flutter’s `in_app_purchase`, change the listener logic to follow this shape:

```dart
Future<void> handlePurchaseUpdates(
  List<PurchaseDetails> purchases,
) async {
  for (final purchase in purchases) {
    switch (purchase.status) {
      case PurchaseStatus.pending:
        premiumState.setPurchasePending(true);
        break;

      case PurchaseStatus.purchased:
      case PurchaseStatus.restored:
        premiumState.setPurchasePending(false);
        await _processCompletedPurchase(purchase);
        break;

      case PurchaseStatus.canceled:
        premiumState.setPurchasePending(false);
        break;

      case PurchaseStatus.error:
        premiumState.setPurchasePending(false);
        premiumState.reportPurchaseError(purchase.error);
        break;
    }
  }
}

Future<void> _processCompletedPurchase(
  PurchaseDetails purchase,
) async {
  final result = await purchaseVerifier.verify(
    productId: purchase.productID,
    store: purchase.verificationData.source,
    verificationData:
        purchase.verificationData.serverVerificationData,
  );

  if (!result.isVerified) {
    premiumState.reportVerificationFailure();
    return;
  }

  // This operation must be idempotent using the verified transaction ID
  // or purchase token. Replayed callbacks must not grant twice.
  await entitlementRepository.applyVerifiedPurchase(
    accountId: authenticatedAccount.id,
    verifiedPurchase: result,
  );

  // Finish only after entitlement delivery has been recorded durably.
  if (purchase.pendingCompletePurchase) {
    await inAppPurchase.completePurchase(purchase);
  }

  await entitlementRepository.refreshEntitlements(
    accountId: authenticatedAccount.id,
  );
}
```

The concrete behavioral changes are:

- `PurchaseStatus.pending` shows a pending state and grants no premium access.
- `purchased` and `restored` transactions pass through the existing trusted verification boundary.
- Premium comes from the verified entitlement repository, rather than the purchase callback or UI state.
- Entitlement delivery is idempotent by transaction ID or Play purchase token.
- `completePurchase` runs only after durable entitlement delivery, allowing recovery if the app stops between verification and acknowledgement.
- Cancelled, failed, malformed, unknown-product, and unverified transactions never unlock premium.
- The purchase listener should have one owner created early in application startup so purchases completed after a restart are processed.
- On account changes, clear cached premium state and reconcile entitlements for the newly authenticated account. Do not attribute an in-flight transaction until its owning account is known.

Do not treat `localVerificationData`, a client-side product ID match, or the Play callback itself as trusted verification. For account-based premium access, send `serverVerificationData` to the existing backend, verify it with Google Play, bind it to the authenticated account, and return the effective entitlement.

Tests should cover pending-to-purchased, pending across restart, duplicate callbacks, verification failure, delivery succeeded but acknowledgement was interrupted, cancellation, restore/reconciliation, offline retry, and account switching. A passing test must confirm both the entitlement state and whether `completePurchase` was called at the correct point.

`softWrap: false` disables wrapping at soft break opportunities; it does **not** suppress explicit `\n` characters or override `maxLines`. Removing `maxLines: 1` allows server copy with newlines to render on multiple lines.

Restore the one-line limit and choose truncation explicitly:

```dart
Text(
  title,
  softWrap: false,
  maxLines: 1,
  overflow: TextOverflow.ellipsis,
)
```

Give the title finite horizontal space—for example, `Expanded` if it shares a bounded `Row` with siblings.

A regression test should render `'First line\nSecond line'` in a narrow container and verify the actual paragraph is limited and truncated:

```dart
testWidgets('title stays one line with server newlines', (tester) async {
  await tester.pumpWidget(
    const MaterialApp(
      home: Scaffold(
        body: SizedBox(
          width: 160,
          child: Text(
            'First line\nSecond line',
            key: ValueKey('title'),
            softWrap: false,
            maxLines: 1,
            overflow: TextOverflow.ellipsis,
          ),
        ),
      ),
    ),
  );

  final richText = find.descendant(
    of: find.byKey(const ValueKey('title')),
    matching: find.byType(RichText),
  );
  final paragraph = tester.renderObject<RenderParagraph>(richText);

  expect(paragraph.maxLines, 1);
  expect(paragraph.overflow, TextOverflow.ellipsis);
  expect(paragraph.didExceedMaxLines, isTrue);
  expect(tester.takeException(), isNull);
});
```

